multipleChoice.questionText:
Consider the code:

String s = "APCSA";
String t = s.substring(2, 5);

What is the value of t after execution?

Given the declaration below, which statement best explains the effect of the code?

String word = "code";
word.toUpperCase();

Consider the code:

Double a = 3.5;
Double b = 2.0;
double c = a + b;

Which statement is correct?

Consider the code:
```java
int score = 82;
String result = "";
if (score >= 90)
{
    result = "A";
}
else if (score >= 80)
{
    result = "B";
}
else if (score >= 70)
{
    result = "C";
}
else
{
    result = "D or F";
}
```
What is the value of $$result$$ after execution?

Consider the code:
```java
int n = 12;
if (n > 0)
{
    if (n % 2 == 0)
    {
        System.out.print("even");
    }
    else
    {
        System.out.print("odd");
    }
}
else
{
    System.out.print("nonpositive");
}
```
What is printed?

Consider the code segment:
```java
int x = 1;
int sum = 0;
while (x <= 7) {
    if (x % 2 == 1) {
        sum += x;
    }
    x += 2;
}
System.out.print(sum);
```
What is printed as a result of executing the code?

A student writes the following code segment to count how many times a value can be divided by $$2$$ before becoming less than $$1$$.
```java
double n = 20.0;
int count = 0;
while (n >= 1) {
    n /= 2;
    count++;
}
```
Which statement best describes why this loop terminates?

Consider the code segment:
```java
int count = 0;
for (int r = 1; r <= 3; r++) {
    for (int c = 1; c <= r; c++) {
        count++;
    }
}
System.out.print(count);
```
What is printed as a result of executing the code?

Consider the method.
```java
public static int countVowels(String str) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < str.length(); i++) {
        String ch = str.substring(i, i + 1);
        if (ch.equals("a") || ch.equals("e") || ch.equals("i") || ch.equals("o") || ch.equals("u")) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```
What value is returned by `countVowels("iteration")` ?

Consider the class below.

```java
public class Book {
    private String title;
    private int pages;

    public Book(String t, int p) {
        title = t;
        pages = p;
    }
}
```
Which statement best describes the effect of the constructor?

A student writes the following class.

```java
public class Counter {
    private int count;

    public Counter() {
        count = 0;
    }

    public void increment() {
        count++;
    }
}
```
Which statement is true about the variable `count`?

Consider the class below.

```java
public class ScoreKeeper {
    private int score;

    public ScoreKeeper() {
        score = 0;
    }

    public int getScore() {
        return score;
    }

    public void addPoints(int pts) {
        score += pts;
    }
}
```
Which statement correctly classifies the methods `getScore` and `addPoints`?

Consider the class below.

```java
public class Tracker {
    private int value;
    private static int totalObjects = 0;

    public Tracker() {
        value = 0;
        totalObjects++;
    }
}
```
Which statement about `totalObjects` is correct?

Consider the following code segment:

```java
int[] values = {4, 7, 1, 9, 2};
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < values.length - 1; i++) {
    sum += values[i];
}
```

What value is stored in `sum` after the code executes?

Consider the following method.

```java
public static int countEven(int[] nums) {
    int count = 0;
    for (int k = 0; k < nums.length; k++) {
        if (nums[k] % 2 == 0) {
            count++;
        }
    }
    return count;
}
```

Which statement best describes the method?

Consider the following method.

```java
public static int mystery(int[] arr, int target) {
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == target) {
            return i;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

Assume `arr` contains no duplicate values. What does the method return?

Consider the following code segment.

```java
int[] data = {5, 3, 8, 1, 6};
int minIndex = 0;
for (int j = 1; j < data.length; j++) {
    if (data[j] < data[minIndex]) {
        minIndex = j;
    }
}
```

What is the value of `minIndex` after the loop finishes?

Consider the code segment:
```java
int[][] values = {
  {1, 2, 3},
  {4, 5, 6}
};
int sum = 0;
for (int r = 0; r < values.length; r++) {
  for (int c = 0; c < values[0].length; c++) {
    sum += values[r][c];
  }
}
```
What is the value of `sum` after execution?

Consider the code segment:
```java
int[][] mat = {
  {3, 1, 4},
  {1, 5, 9},
  {2, 6, 5}
};
int total = 0;
for (int c = 0; c < mat[0].length; c++) {
  total += mat[1][c];
}
```
What value is stored in `total` after execution?

A programmer writes the following code for a possibly jagged array `int[][] arr`:
```java
for (int r = 0; r < arr.length; r++) {
  for (int c = 0; c < arr[0].length; c++) {
    System.out.println(arr[r][c]);
  }
}
```
Why can this code fail for some valid `arr` values?

Consider the method below. What value is returned by $$f(4)$$?

```java
public static int f(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return 2;
    }
    return f(n - 1) + n;
}
```

Consider the method below. What is returned by $$mystery(1234)$$?

```java
public static int mystery(int n) {
    if (n < 10) {
        return n;
    }
    return mystery(n / 10);
}
```

Consider the method below. What is printed when $$show(3)$$ is called?

```java
public static void show(int n) {
    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }
    System.out.print(n + " ");
    show(n - 1);
    System.out.print(n + " ");
}
```

fillInTheBlank.questionText:
In the method below, the recursive call removes the last digit, so the value returned by $$sumDigits(508)$$ is ______.

```java
public static int sumDigits(int n) {
    if (n < 10) {
        return n;
    }
    return n % 10 + sumDigits(n / 10);
}
```
